---
layout: app-shell.njk
permalink: /f1/
title: "Formula 1 | Stefanos Tzortzoglou"
shell: true
bodyClass: "bg-brand-bg text-brand-text antialiased min-h-screen flex flex-col"
navDrawerOnly: true
signOut: true
bodyScripts:
  - src: "/scripts/f1-page.js"
    type: "module"
  - src: "/scripts/contact-widget.js"
    type: "module"
  - src: "/scripts/palette.js"
    type: "module"
---
<section class="app-page">

  <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-4 mb-2">
    <h1 class="flex items-center gap-3 text-3xl font-bold text-brand-text">
      {% icon "fa-solid-flag-checkered", "h-7 w-7 text-brand-accent" %}
      <span>Formula 1</span>
      <span id="f1Season" class="text-brand-muted font-normal"></span>
    </h1>

    <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
      <label for="roundPick" class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Round</label>
      <select id="roundPick" class="field w-auto py-1.5 text-sm">
        <option>Loading…</option>
      </select>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div id="status" role="status" aria-live="polite" class="mb-6 text-sm"></div>

  {# --- the chosen round ---------------------------------------------------
     Sessions when it has not run, results when it has, and both mid-weekend - which is the
     state the round selector exists to make reachable. #}
  <section aria-labelledby="round-heading" class="mb-10">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4 gap-y-1 mb-1">
      <h2 id="round-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text"></h2>
      <p id="roundWhere" class="text-sm text-brand-muted"></p>
    </div>
    <div id="roundBody"></div>
  </section>

  {# --- where in the world -------------------------------------------------
     A globe rather than a place name, because the thing worth seeing about a calendar is how far
     it throws itself around the world between one round and the next. Every round of the season
     is a dot and the chosen one is lit; changing the round turns the globe to it.

     The map opens framed to the chosen circuit's own outline, traced from OpenStreetMap, because
     at that range a dot has stopped saying which place this is and the shape of the track is the
     only thing that distinguishes one from the next. Framed to the shape and not set to a zoom:
     Monaco is 3.3 km of street and Spa is 7 km through a forest, so any one number is too close
     for one and too far for the other, and the box each outline already carries solves for the
     number that fits it. Zooming out is how you get the world back, and the flight between two
     rounds arcs out through it on the way.

     The dots stay: they are what keeps the chosen round findable at every zoom, and the outline
     is an addition to them rather than a replacement for them.

     **Satellite is the other half of the same answer, and it is off until asked for.** A drawn
     map shows a purpose-built circuit as a road like any other; a photograph shows Spa as a pale
     ribbon through a forest and Monza as an oval nobody could mistake. It does nothing for Monaco
     or Baku, which are public streets from above as well as on the ground - and that is what the
     outline over the top is for. The imagery is a second host, so it is fetched only once the
     button is pressed, and the privacy notice says who serves it. /transit/ has no such button:
     photography hides the street name and the stop label, which is that map's whole job.

     The dots are drawn into a canvas and no screen reader can reach them, so the canvas is hidden
     from one and the round selector at the top of the page is their text alternative - it names
     every round of the season, which is exactly what the dots are.

     The height is explicit because MapLibre measures its container: without one it renders a
     canvas zero pixels tall, which looks like a map that failed rather than a map with no room.
     It is also generous, and that is the point of a globe: a sphere loses its edges to a short
     box, and a circuit you have zoomed into needs room to be a circuit rather than a squiggle. #}
  <section aria-labelledby="map-heading" class="mb-10">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-x-4 gap-y-1 mb-3">
      <h2 id="map-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text">Where it is raced</h2>
      <p id="circuitName" class="text-sm text-brand-muted"></p>
    </div>
    {# A flex column whose map is the part that grows, because the fullscreen button hands this
       whole card to the browser rather than the map box inside it - the key below is the picture's
       text and it has to come along. At the card's ordinary auto height there is no free space to
       distribute, so the map keeps exactly the height its own classes give it; fullscreen makes the
       card viewport-tall and the one growing child takes everything the key and the note do not. #}
    <div class="card flex flex-col p-3">
      <div id="f1Map" class="h-96 w-full grow overflow-hidden rounded-lg sm:h-[32rem]"></div>

      {# The key. The dots are drawn inside a canvas no screen reader can enter, so this is not a
         decoration of the picture - it is the picture's text, and it says the same things the
         colours and the shapes do. Static markup rather than drawn by the script: it describes a
         scheme that is fixed, and a legend that only appears once a map has loaded is missing
         exactly when somebody is trying to work out why the map has not. #}
      <ul class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-5 gap-y-2 px-2 pb-1 text-xs text-brand-muted">
        <li class="flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="h-2.5 w-2.5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-brand-hover ring-2 ring-brand-surface"
            aria-hidden="true"></span>the round shown here
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="h-2 w-2 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-brand-accent" aria-hidden="true"></span>still to come
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="h-2 w-2 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-brand-muted opacity-70" aria-hidden="true"></span>already raced
        </li>
        {# The outline takes its colour from the same raced/still-to-come flag as the dot beneath
           it, so this row describes a shape and not a fourth colour - and says so, because a
           swatch in the accent beside the word "circuit" would otherwise read as a fourth state.

           This row used to end "once you zoom in", which was true and was the bug: the map opened
           at zoom 4 and the outline fades in between 8 and 11, so it was never once seen without
           a reader going looking for it. The map now arrives framed to the circuit. The line is
           still the first thing to go when you pull back, which is what the row says instead. #}
        <li class="flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="h-0.5 w-5 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-brand-accent"
            aria-hidden="true"></span>the circuit itself, in those same two colours, until you zoom out
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p id="mapNote" class="hint mt-3 px-2 pb-1" hidden></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {# --- championships ------------------------------------------------------ #}
  <section aria-labelledby="standings-heading">
    <h2 id="standings-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">Championships</h2>
    <p id="standingsAfter" class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4"></p>
    <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text mb-2">Drivers</h3>
        <ul id="driverStandings" class="card divide-y divide-brand-border"></ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-brand-text mb-2">Constructors</h3>
        <ul id="teamStandings" class="card divide-y divide-brand-border"></ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</section>
